          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "**What it is:** Hypertension, or high blood pressure, means pressure in the arteries stays raised over time rather than on one occasion. Blood pressure has two numbers: the systolic (when the heart beats) over the diastolic (between beats), both in millimeters of mercury. It usually causes no symptoms and is most often found during routine measurement. (*Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference)\n\n**One high reading vs. a pattern:** Pressure changes through the day with activity, stress, caffeine, sleep and time of day. A single high reading in a clinic is therefore not a diagnosis. Diagnosis rests on repeated measurements. (*Hypertension (high blood pressure)*, general clinical reference)\n\n**How office readings are classified (adults, US society guidance):**\n- **Normal:** systolic below 120 and diastolic below 80.\n- **Elevated:** systolic 120 to 129 and diastolic below 80.\n- **Stage 1 hypertension:** systolic 130 to 139 or diastolic 80 to 89.\n- **Stage 2 hypertension:** systolic 140 or higher or diastolic 90 or higher.\n\nIf the two numbers fall in different categories, the higher one applies. (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*, cardiology guidance)\n\nThe sources I searched do not say how many readings, or over what period, are needed to confirm a diagnosis. A clinician would decide that. If you have repeatedly high readings, that is a good reason to raise it at your next appointment.\n\n**A note on safety:** Seek emergency care right away if a very high reading comes with chest pain, severe breathlessness, weakness on one side, confusion or a severe headache. This is general safety advice, not from the reference material I searched.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
